      <h2>Why "Save for Web (Legacy)" Fails to Solve the Problem</h2>
      <p>
        Many Photoshop users assume that selecting <em>File &gt; Export &gt; Save for Web (Legacy)</em> automatically strips unnecessary metadata. However, Adobe engineering has tied certain metadata retention behaviors to color profiles and copyright requirements:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Metadata Dropdown Pitfalls:</strong> If the metadata dropdown is set to <code>Copyright and Contact Info</code> or <code>All</code>, Photoshop includes the entire XMP packet, preserving the full multi-megabyte <code>DocumentAncestors</code> payload.</li>
        <li><strong>Retained Photoshop 3.0 8BIM Headers:</strong> Even when set to <code>None</code>, Photoshop frequently embeds legacy 8BIM resource blocks that retain layer history and software licensing serial hashes.</li>
        <li><strong>Mobile and Web App Crashes:</strong> When bloated JPEGs are uploaded to Discord, Shopify, or WordPress, backend servers frequently crash or reject uploads due to exceeding PHP memory limits or maximum header limits.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Camera Raw Presets and Editing Recipes: What <code>crs:</code> Tags Leak</h2>
      <p>
        In addition to DocumentAncestors bloat, exports from Adobe Camera Raw and Adobe Lightroom embed extensive adjustment parameters within <code>crs:</code> metadata tags. These parameters describe every slider position, color grading curve, local brush mask, and retouching coordinate used during post-production.
      </p>
      <p>
        For commercial photographers and digital artists, these tags leak proprietary color grading recipes to clients and competitors. RemoveSynth detects and strips all <code>crs:Temperature</code>, <code>crs:Tint</code>, <code>crs:ToneCurve</code>, and <code>crs:Look</code> tags, protecting your creative workflow.
      </p>

      <h2>Step-by-Step Guide: Cleaning Photoshop JPEGs in 3 Clicks</h2>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><strong>Upload Photoshop export:</strong> Drop your bloated Photoshop JPG, PNG, or WebP files into the cleaner box above.</li>
        <li><strong>Client-side XMP excision:</strong> The tool parses the APP1 marker and excises photoshop:DocumentAncestors, xmpMM:History, and crs presets in browser RAM.</li>
        <li><strong>Download clean image:</strong> Save the optimized file instantly with 100 percent identical image quality and zero server uploads.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Empirical Size Reduction Benchmarks</h2>
      <p>
        The table below documents actual file size savings across common graphic assets exported from Adobe Creative Cloud software:
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
